rrorist attack but fear of violence  persists in this country possibly  because of breathless media coverage of  terrorism and mass shootings that fails  to put the magnitude of the problem in  proper context think about the next  generation do you think they'll have it  better or worse than we do  I think worse as far as economy and all  that things are becoming more or less  affordable not becoming very affordable  starting with food unemployment may be  back to around 5% but that's partly  because many Americans have dropped out  of the labor market altogether so it's  no surprise that they're pessimistic  about  economy but there's still reason for  optimism in this realm if historical  trends hold if you break the income of  Americans into quintiles and think of  them as rungs on the economic ladder one  study of US Treasury data found that 95  percent of families on the lowest rung  moved up at least one wrong in their  lifetime with a majority actually moving  to the top two rungs and 82 percent of  kids whose parents were on the bottom  rung moved up at least one rung not only  that but the bang for your buck is  increasing the amount of hours the  poorest Americans have to work to afford  household appliances and luxury items  has steadily declined since the 1950s  like back in the 50s you get to work  four times as many hours just to buy a  refrigerator as today that's very very  exciting I do see there are generation  we do work glass we work less we're a  little get a little more out of it more  yes we make the most of our time what if  I told you that among even the poorest  Americans over the last 40 years there's  been a 28% increase in purchasing power  that is uplifting I mean that does help  it's you know better for the people now  let's move back to the big loud orange  elephant in the room for a moment a big  beautiful powerful wall when I see  politicians stand in front of people and  make fun of others and say horrible  things and then I see in